eing them in context if i  look at a table of these there's no way  i can remember them all but if i see  them in different contexts and then i  question like why is this this and then  eventually you start to get used to them  but  you know if you have like yasui yasui is  inexpensive  like kakudaro probably will write yasui  inexpensive  even nouns  [Music]  so that basically i don't see them as  uh sort of verb endings per se i just  see them as  sort of  suffixes or  column endings  that have meanings  that are attached to verbs nouns  whatever  uh  and there's an unlimited number of them  and what's delightful in japanese is you  can say um  so that you can also the kaku can  sometimes become kaitara kakimashita  kakunaraba  is another way of saying it so that  there's this variety it seems to me in  japanese there's  two or three different ways of arriving  at the same result  we can look at a table of these in fact  it might be useful to get some kind of  an overall comprehensive sense of how  all this stuff works  but ultimately you just have to  expose yourself to enough of the  of the language in meaningful context  where you start to see these i'm trying  to think of some other uh  rabbis because  i don't know why but the naraba is there  again  i mean it's just all kinds of ways in  japanese and every language has sort of  that  almost  sensual delight that we get in using it  using the sort of idiosyncrasies of the  language in the case of japanese it's  playing with all these different  things that get stacked onto the end of  words to shape what we're trying to say  to put in  a certain amount of uh  again so typically japanese some  hesitation and a  chance to think about what you're going  to say and those are the delights of the  japanese language i don't see them as  the same kind of problem as case endings  in russian or in the slavic languages  where  you know ooh could be this case for this  gender and it could be something else  for some other